  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The model's performance and training feedback loops were evaluated solely within a simulated environment of a single intersection, which may not capture the complexity of real-world driver behavior or network-wide traffic dynamics.",
      "what": "The study develops a joint optimization model that combines a multiagent deep Q-network (MADQN) for CAV trajectory control with an adaptive queue-pressure signal control strategy. The model was evaluated using simulation experiments on a real-world multidirectional intersection, testing performance across varying CAV penetration rates using a global reward function that minimizes average delay and energy consumption.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that coordinating vehicle trajectories and signal phases simultaneously can reduce traffic delays even at low CAV penetration rates, providing a pathway to incremental traffic efficiency gains during the transition to fully automated fleets."
    }
